      <p><strong>The Lawrence Home</strong></p>
      <p><strong>311 6th Ave NW</strong></p>

      <p>
        The Lawrence home was built in 1938 on land Harold Lawrence purchased
        from the Northern Pacific Railroad. Harold was married to Frances
        Lawrence (her maiden name unknown) and had one son, Robert.
      </p>

      <p>
        Harold owned Lawrence’s Men’s Wear on Main Street in Mandan for many
        years. The current homeowner can remember buying clothes for her
        children at Lawrence’s Men’s Wear when they were young. Harold was
        active in the community and took part in various civic activities. He
        was also a member of the Rotary Club for many years.
      </p>

      <p>
        Frances endured much tragedy as a wife and mother. She lost her only
        son in a car accident four years after he graduated from Mandan High
        School. Then lost her husband to a tragic murder by strangulation in
        the Men’s Wear store when Harold was 69 years old. The killer was never
        identified, and, even though there were several suspects, no arrest was
        ever made. Most of the people with information pertinent to the murder
        have now passed on, but Deputy Police Chief Dennis Bullinger recalls
        the murder well and investigated it extensively.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even though Frances endured much tragedy from the loss of her son and
        husband, she continued to live in the home her husband built. Research
        though state archives indicates that Frances passed away in 1976, four
        years after her husband. She was 66 years old at the time of her death.
        The entire family is buried in Mandan’s Union Cemetery.
      </p>

      <p>
        The current homeowner, Terry Piper, has lived in the home since 1978.
        She and her children have brought much happiness to a grief stricken
        home. Terry has six grandchildren who enjoy visiting the home.
      </p>

      <p>
        There has been some updating to the exterior of the home, however, the
        interior retains the original woodwork from when it was built. This
        home is a beautiful historical home with just a hint of mystery.
      </p>

      <p class="source-credit">Draft transcription from the photographed sign; it has not yet received a second review.</p>
